🔄 Typecasting in Java
Typecasting in Java means converting a variable from one data type to another. It is mainly of two types:

✅ 1. Widening (Implicit) Typecasting
Smaller type → larger type

Done automatically by the compiler

No data loss

🔸 Example:

int a = 10;
double b = a;  // int to double (automatic)
System.out.println(b);  // Output: 10.0
| From  | To     |
| ----- | ------ |
| byte  | short  |
| short | int    |
| int   | long   |
| long  | float  |
| float | double |


⚠️ 2. Narrowing (Explicit) Typecasting
Larger type → smaller type

You must cast manually

Risk of data loss

🔸 Example:

double a = 10.99;
int b = (int) a;  // double to int
System.out.println(b);  // Output: 10
💡 Why Use Typecasting?
When you're working with mixed data types (e.g., int + float)

When converting object types (upcasting/downcasting)

For performance optimizations (in low-level programming)

🎯 Bonus: Typecasting in Objects (OOP)
🔹 Upcasting (Superclass ← Subclass)

Dog d = new Dog();
Animal a = d;  // upcasting (automatic)
🔹 Downcasting (Subclass ← Superclass)

Animal a = new Dog();
Dog d = (Dog) a;  // downcasting (manual)
